        <body>
            <div xml:id="d736">

                <pb facs="0707" n="695" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg695"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A Armed Merchantmen/5</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Canadian Legation to the Department of State </hi>
                </head>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Aide-Mémoire</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The <hi rendition="#italic">Lady</hi> class ships of the Canadian National
                    Steamships ply between Bermuda and Halifax, Nova Scotia, calling at Boston,
                    Massachusetts. The Canadian authorities have installed in each of these vessels
                    a gun for defensive armament against submarines. In addition these ships may
                    from time to time carry naval stores, e. g. ammunition, from Bermuda to Halifax,
                    calling at Boston en route.</p>
                <p>In connection with calls at United States ports by Canadian ships either with
                    defensive armament or munitions on board, the Canadian Government are most
                    anxious that Canadian ships should comply with all regulations which United
                    States authorities may impose with respect to such vessels. The Canadian
                    Legation would, therefore, <pb facs="0708" n="696" xml:id="pg696"/> appreciate
                    learning what requirements if any, must be met by such vessels when calling at
                    ports of the United States.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">[<placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-26">September 26,
                            1939</date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
